FILE management-style.html
191: Linux kernel management style¶
192: This is a short document describing the preferred (or made up, depending
193: on who you ask) management style for the linux kernel.  It’s meant to
194: mirror the process/coding-style.rst document to some
195: degree, and mainly written to avoid answering [1]  the same (or similar)
196: questions over and over again.
197: Management style is very personal and much harder to quantify than
198: simple coding style rules, so this document may or may not have anything
199: to do with reality.  It started as a lark, but that doesn’t mean that it
200: might not actually be true. You’ll have to decide for yourself.
201: Btw, when talking about “kernel manager”, it’s all about the technical
202: lead persons, not the people who do traditional management inside
203: companies.  If you sign purchase orders or you have any clue about the
204: budget of your group, you’re almost certainly not a kernel manager.
205: These suggestions may or may not apply to you.
206: First off, I’d suggest buying “Seven Habits of Highly Effective
207: People”, and NOT read it.  Burn it, it’s a great symbolic gesture.
210: [1]
211: This document does so not so much by answering the question, but by
212: making it painfully obvious to the questioner that we don’t have a clue
213: to what the answer is.
216: Anyway, here goes:
218: 1) Decisions¶
219: Everybody thinks managers make decisions, and that decision-making is
220: important.  The bigger and more painful the decision, the bigger the
221: manager must be to make it.  That’s very deep and obvious, but it’s not
222: actually true.
223: The name of the game is to avoid having to make a decision.  In
224: particular, if somebody tells you “choose (a) or (b), we really need you
225: to decide on this”, you’re in trouble as a manager.  The people you
226: manage had better know the details better than you, so if they come to
227: you for a technical decision, you’re screwed.  You’re clearly not
228: competent to make that decision for them.
229: (Corollary:if the people you manage don’t know the details better than
230: you, you’re also screwed, although for a totally different reason.
231: Namely that you are in the wrong job, and that they should be managing
232: your brilliance instead).
233: So the name of the game is to avoid decisions, at least the big and
234: painful ones.  Making small and non-consequential decisions is fine, and
235: makes you look like you know what you’re doing, so what a kernel manager
236: needs to do is to turn the big and painful ones into small things where
237: nobody really cares.
238: It helps to realize that the key difference between a big decision and a
239: small one is whether you can fix your decision afterwards.  Any decision
240: can be made small by just always making sure that if you were wrong (and
241: you will be wrong), you can always undo the damage later by
242: backtracking.  Suddenly, you get to be doubly managerial for making
243: two inconsequential decisions - the wrong one and the right one.
244: And people will even see that as true leadership (cough bullshit
245: cough).
246: Thus the key to avoiding big decisions becomes to just avoiding to do
247: things that can’t be undone.  Don’t get ushered into a corner from which
248: you cannot escape.  A cornered rat may be dangerous - a cornered manager
249: is just pitiful.
250: It turns out that since nobody would be stupid enough to ever really let
251: a kernel manager have huge fiscal responsibility anyway, it’s usually
252: fairly easy to backtrack.  Since you’re not going to be able to waste
253: huge amounts of money that you might not be able to repay, the only
254: thing you can backtrack on is a technical decision, and there
255: back-tracking is very easy: just tell everybody that you were an
256: incompetent nincompoop, say you’re sorry, and undo all the worthless
257: work you had people work on for the last year.  Suddenly the decision
258: you made a year ago wasn’t a big decision after all, since it could be
259: easily undone.
260: It turns out that some people have trouble with this approach, for two
261: reasons:
264: admitting you were an idiot is harder than it looks.  We all like to
265: maintain appearances, and coming out in public to say that you were
266: wrong is sometimes very hard indeed.
267: having somebody tell you that what you worked on for the last year
268: wasn’t worthwhile after all can be hard on the poor lowly engineers
269: too, and while the actual work was easy enough to undo by just
270: deleting it, you may have irrevocably lost the trust of that
271: engineer.  And remember: “irrevocable” was what we tried to avoid in
272: the first place, and your decision ended up being a big one after
273: all.
276: Happily, both of these reasons can be mitigated effectively by just
277: admitting up-front that you don’t have a friggin’ clue, and telling
278: people ahead of the fact that your decision is purely preliminary, and
279: might be the wrong thing.  You should always reserve the right to change
280: your mind, and make people very aware of that.  And it’s much easier
281: to admit that you are stupid when you haven’t yet done the really
282: stupid thing.
283: Then, when it really does turn out to be stupid, people just roll their
284: eyes and say “Oops, not again”.
285: This preemptive admission of incompetence might also make the people who
286: actually do the work also think twice about whether it’s worth doing or
287: not.  After all, if they aren’t certain whether it’s a good idea, you
288: sure as hell shouldn’t encourage them by promising them that what they
289: work on will be included.  Make them at least think twice before they
290: embark on a big endeavor.
291: Remember: they’d better know more about the details than you do, and
292: they usually already think they have the answer to everything.  The best
293: thing you can do as a manager is not to instill confidence, but rather a
294: healthy dose of critical thinking on what they do.
295: Btw, another way to avoid a decision is to plaintively just whine “can’t
296: we just do both?” and look pitiful.  Trust me, it works.  If it’s not
297: clear which approach is better, they’ll eventually figure it out.  The
298: answer may end up being that both teams get so frustrated by the
299: situation that they just give up.
300: That may sound like a failure, but it’s usually a sign that there was
301: something wrong with both projects, and the reason the people involved
302: couldn’t decide was that they were both wrong.  You end up coming up
303: smelling like roses, and you avoided yet another decision that you could
304: have screwed up on.
307: 2) People¶
308: Most people are idiots, and being a manager means you’ll have to deal
309: with it, and perhaps more importantly, that they have to deal with
310: you.
311: It turns out that while it’s easy to undo technical mistakes, it’s not
312: as easy to undo personality disorders.  You just have to live with
313: theirs - and yours.
314: However, in order to prepare yourself as a kernel manager, it’s best to
315: remember not to burn any bridges, bomb any innocent villagers, or
316: alienate too many kernel developers. It turns out that alienating people
317: is fairly easy, and un-alienating them is hard. Thus “alienating”
318: immediately falls under the heading of “not reversible”, and becomes a
319: no-no according to 1) Decisions.
320: There’s just a few simple rules here:
323: don’t call people d*ckheads (at least not in public)
324: learn how to apologize when you forgot rule (1)
327: The problem with #1 is that it’s very easy to do, since you can say
328: “you’re a d*ckhead” in millions of different ways [2], sometimes without
329: even realizing it, and almost always with a white-hot conviction that
330: you are right.
331: And the more convinced you are that you are right (and let’s face it,
332: you can call just about anybody a d*ckhead, and you often will be
333: right), the harder it ends up being to apologize afterwards.
334: To solve this problem, you really only have two options:
337: get really good at apologies
338: spread the “love” out so evenly that nobody really ends up feeling
339: like they get unfairly targeted.  Make it inventive enough, and they
340: might even be amused.
343: The option of being unfailingly polite really doesn’t exist. Nobody will
344: trust somebody who is so clearly hiding their true character.
347: [2]
348: Paul Simon sang “Fifty Ways to Leave Your Lover”, because quite
349: frankly, “A Million Ways to Tell a Developer They’re a D*ckhead” doesn’t
350: scan nearly as well.  But I’m sure he thought about it.
355: 3) People II - the Good Kind¶
356: While it turns out that most people are idiots, the corollary to that is
357: sadly that you are one too, and that while we can all bask in the secure
358: knowledge that we’re better than the average person (let’s face it,
359: nobody ever believes that they’re average or below-average), we should
360: also admit that we’re not the sharpest knife around, and there will be
361: other people that are less of an idiot than you are.
362: Some people react badly to smart people.  Others take advantage of them.
363: Make sure that you, as a kernel maintainer, are in the second group.
364: Suck up to them, because they are the people who will make your job
365: easier. In particular, they’ll be able to make your decisions for you,
366: which is what the game is all about.
367: So when you find somebody smarter than you are, just coast along.  Your
368: management responsibilities largely become ones of saying “Sounds like a
369: good idea - go wild”, or “That sounds good, but what about xxx?”.  The
370: second version in particular is a great way to either learn something
371: new about “xxx” or seem extra managerial by pointing out something the
372: smarter person hadn’t thought about.  In either case, you win.
373: One thing to look out for is to realize that greatness in one area does
374: not necessarily translate to other areas.  So you might prod people in
375: specific directions, but let’s face it, they might be good at what they
376: do, and suck at everything else.  The good news is that people tend to
377: naturally gravitate back to what they are good at, so it’s not like you
378: are doing something irreversible when you do prod them in some
379: direction, just don’t push too hard.
382: 4) Placing blame¶
383: Things will go wrong, and people want somebody to blame. Tag, you’re it.
384: It’s not actually that hard to accept the blame, especially if people
385: kind of realize that it wasn’t all your fault.  Which brings us to the
386: best way of taking the blame: do it for someone else. You’ll feel good
387: for taking the fall, they’ll feel good about not getting blamed, and the
388: person who lost their whole 36GB porn-collection because of your
389: incompetence will grudgingly admit that you at least didn’t try to weasel
390: out of it.
391: Then make the developer who really screwed up (if you can find them) know
392: in private that they screwed up.  Not just so they can avoid it in the
393: future, but so that they know they owe you one.  And, perhaps even more
394: importantly, they’re also likely the person who can fix it.  Because, let’s
395: face it, it sure ain’t you.
396: Taking the blame is also why you get to be manager in the first place.
397: It’s part of what makes people trust you, and allow you the potential
398: glory, because you’re the one who gets to say “I screwed up”.  And if
399: you’ve followed the previous rules, you’ll be pretty good at saying that
400: by now.
403: 5) Things to avoid¶
404: There’s one thing people hate even more than being called “d*ckhead”,
405: and that is being called a “d*ckhead” in a sanctimonious voice.  The
406: first you can apologize for, the second one you won’t really get the
407: chance.  They likely will no longer be listening even if you otherwise
408: do a good job.
409: We all think we’re better than anybody else, which means that when
410: somebody else puts on airs, it really rubs us the wrong way.  You may
411: be morally and intellectually superior to everybody around you, but
412: don’t try to make it too obvious unless you really intend to irritate
413: somebody [3].
414: Similarly, don’t be too polite or subtle about things. Politeness easily
415: ends up going overboard and hiding the problem, and as they say, “On the
416: internet, nobody can hear you being subtle”. Use a big blunt object to
417: hammer the point in, because you can’t really depend on people getting
418: your point otherwise.
419: Some humor can help pad both the bluntness and the moralizing.  Going
420: overboard to the point of being ridiculous can drive a point home
421: without making it painful to the recipient, who just thinks you’re being
422: silly.  It can thus help get through the personal mental block we all
423: have about criticism.
426: [3]
427: Hint: internet newsgroups that are not directly related to your work
428: are great ways to take out your frustrations at other people. Write
429: insulting posts with a sneer just to get into a good flame every once in
430: a while, and you’ll feel cleansed. Just don’t crap too close to home.
435: 6) Why me?¶
436: Since your main responsibility seems to be to take the blame for other
437: peoples mistakes, and make it painfully obvious to everybody else that
438: you’re incompetent, the obvious question becomes one of why do it in the
439: first place?
440: First off, while you may or may not get screaming teenage girls (or
441: boys, let’s not be judgmental or sexist here) knocking on your dressing
442: room door, you will get an immense feeling of personal accomplishment
443: for being “in charge”.  Never mind the fact that you’re really leading
444: by trying to keep up with everybody else and running after them as fast
445: as you can.  Everybody will still think you’re the person in charge.
446: It’s a great job if you can hack it.

