202: a block of control starts and ends.  Especially when you’ve been looking
203: at your screen for 20 straight hours, you’ll find it a lot easier to see
204: how the indentation works if you have large indentations.
205: Now, some people will claim that having 8-character indentations makes
206: the code move too far to the right, and makes it hard to read on a
207: 80-character terminal screen.  The answer to that is that if you need
208: more than 3 levels of indentation, you’re screwed anyway, and should fix
209: your program.
210: In short, 8-char indents make things easier to read, and have the added
211: benefit of warning you when you’re nesting your functions too deep.
212: Heed that warning.
213: The preferred way to ease multiple indentation levels in a switch statement is
214: to align the switch and its subordinate case labels in the same column
215: instead of double-indenting the case labels.  E.g.:
216: switch (suffix) {
217: case 'G':
218: case 'g':
219:         mem <<= 30;
220:         break;
221: case 'M':
222: case 'm':
223:         mem <<= 20;
224:         break;
225: case 'K':
226: case 'k':
227:         mem <<= 10;
228:         fallthrough;
229: default:
230:         break;
231: }
234: Don’t put multiple statements on a single line unless you have
235: something to hide:
236: if (condition) do_this;
237:   do_something_everytime;
240: Don’t use commas to avoid using braces:
241: if (condition)
242:         do_this(), do_that();
245: Always use braces for multiple statements:
246: if (condition) {
247:         do_this();
248:         do_that();
249: }
252: Don’t put multiple assignments on a single line either.  Kernel coding style
253: is super simple.  Avoid tricky expressions.
254: Outside of comments, documentation and except in Kconfig, spaces are never
255: used for indentation, and the above example is deliberately broken.
256: Get a decent editor and don’t leave whitespace at the end of lines.
259: 2) Breaking long lines and strings¶
260: Coding style is all about readability and maintainability using commonly
261: available tools.
262: The preferred limit on the length of a single line is 80 columns.
263: Statements longer than 80 columns should be broken into sensible chunks,
264: unless exceeding 80 columns significantly increases readability and does
265: not hide information.
266: Descendants are always substantially shorter than the parent and
267: are placed substantially to the right.  A very commonly used style
268: is to align descendants to a function open parenthesis.
269: These same rules are applied to function headers with a long argument list.
270: However, never break user-visible strings such as printk messages because
271: that breaks the ability to grep for them.
418: Git will warn you about patches that introduce trailing whitespace, and can
419: optionally strip the trailing whitespace for you; however, if applying a series
420: of patches, this may make later patches in the series fail by changing their
421: context lines.
425: 4) Naming¶
426: C is a Spartan language, and your naming conventions should follow suit.
427: Unlike Modula-2 and Pascal programmers, C programmers do not use cute
428: names like ThisVariableIsATemporaryCounter. A C programmer would call that
429: variable tmp, which is much easier to write, and not the least more
430: difficult to understand.
431: HOWEVER, while mixed-case names are frowned upon, descriptive names for
432: global variables are a must.  To call a global function foo is a
433: shooting offense.
434: GLOBAL variables (to be used only if you really need them) need to
435: have descriptive names, as do global functions.  If you have a function
436: that counts the number of active users, you should call that
437: count_active_users() or similar, you should not call it cntusr().
438: Encoding the type of a function into the name (so-called Hungarian
439: notation) is asinine - the compiler knows the types anyway and can check
440: those, and it only confuses the programmer.
441: LOCAL variable names should be short, and to the point.  If you have
442: some random integer loop counter, it should probably be called i.
443: Calling it loop_counter is non-productive, if there is no chance of it
444: being mis-understood.  Similarly, tmp can be just about any type of
445: variable that is used to hold a temporary value.
446: If you are afraid to mix up your local variable names, you have another
447: problem, which is called the function-growth-hormone-imbalance syndrome.
448: See chapter 6 (Functions).
531: with userspace.
535: Maybe there are other cases too, but the rule should basically be to NEVER
536: EVER use a typedef unless you can clearly match one of those rules.
537: In general, a pointer, or a struct that has elements that can reasonably
538: be directly accessed should never be a typedef.
541: 6) Functions¶
542: Functions should be short and sweet, and do just one thing.  They should
543: fit on one or two screenfuls of text (the ISO/ANSI screen size is 80x24,
544: as we all know), and do one thing and do that well.
545: The maximum length of a function is inversely proportional to the
546: complexity and indentation level of that function.  So, if you have a
547: conceptually simple function that is just one long (but simple)
548: case-statement, where you have to do lots of small things for a lot of
549: different cases, it’s OK to have a longer function.
550: However, if you have a complex function, and you suspect that a
551: less-than-gifted first-year high-school student might not even
552: understand what the function is all about, you should adhere to the
553: maximum limits all the more closely.  Use helper functions with
554: descriptive names (you can ask the compiler to in-line them if you think
555: it’s performance-critical, and it will probably do a better job of it
556: than you would have done).
557: Another measure of the function is the number of local variables.  They
558: shouldn’t exceed 5-10, or you’re doing something wrong.  Re-think the
559: function, and split it into smaller pieces.  A human brain can
560: generally easily keep track of about 7 different things, anything more
561: and it gets confused.  You know you’re brilliant, but maybe you’d like
562: to understand what you did 2 weeks from now.
563: In source files, separate functions with one blank line.  If the function is
564: exported, the EXPORT macro for it should follow immediately after the
565: closing function brace line.  E.g.:
566: int system_is_up(void)
567: {
568:         return system_state == SYSTEM_RUNNING;
569: }
570: EXPORT_SYMBOL(system_is_up);
574: 6.1) Function prototypes¶
575: In function prototypes, include parameter names with their data types.
576: Although this is not required by the C language, it is preferred in Linux
577: because it is a simple way to add valuable information for the reader.
578: Do not use the extern keyword with function declarations as this makes
579: lines longer and isn’t strictly necessary.
580: When writing function prototypes, please keep the order of elements regular.
581: For example, using this function declaration example:
582: __init void * __must_check action(enum magic value, size_t size, u8 count,
583:                                   char *fmt, ...) __printf(4, 5) __malloc;
586: The preferred order of elements for a function prototype is:
588: storage class (below, static __always_inline, noting that __always_inline
589: is technically an attribute but is treated like inline)
590: storage class attributes (here, __init -- i.e. section declarations, but also
591: things like __cold)
592: return type (here, void *)
593: return type attributes (here, __must_check)
594: function name (here, action)
595: function parameters (here, (enum magic value, size_t size, u8 count, char *fmt, ...),
596: noting that parameter names should always be included)
652:         kfree(buffer);
653:         return result;
654: }
657: A common type of bug to be aware of is one err bugs which look like this:
658: err:
659:         kfree(foo->bar);
660:         kfree(foo);
661:         return ret;
664: The bug in this code is that on some exit paths foo is NULL.  Normally the
665: fix for this is to split it up into two error labels err_free_bar: and
666: err_free_foo::
667: err_free_bar:
668:         kfree(foo->bar);
669: err_free_foo:
670:         kfree(foo);
671:         return ret;
674: Ideally you should simulate errors to test all exit paths.
677: 8) Commenting¶
678: Comments are good, but there is also a danger of over-commenting.  NEVER
679: try to explain HOW your code works in a comment: it’s much better to
680: write the code so that the working is obvious, and it’s a waste of
681: time to explain badly written code.
682: Generally, you want your comments to tell WHAT your code does, not HOW.
683: Also, try to avoid putting comments inside a function body: if the
684: function is so complex that you need to separately comment parts of it,
685: you should probably go back to chapter 6 for a while.  You can make
686: small comments to note or warn about something particularly clever (or
687: ugly), but try to avoid excess.  Instead, put the comments at the head
688: of the function, telling people what it does, and possibly WHY it does
689: it.
690: When commenting the kernel API functions, please use the kernel-doc format.
691: See the files at Documentation/doc-guide/ and
692: tools/docs/kernel-doc for details. Note that the danger of over-commenting
693: applies to kernel-doc comments all the same. Do not add boilerplate
694: kernel-doc which simply reiterates what’s obvious from the signature
695: of the function.
696: The preferred style for long (multi-line) comments is:
697: /*
698:  * This is the preferred style for multi-line
699:  * comments in the Linux kernel source code.
700:  * Please use it consistently.
701:  *
702:  * Description:  A column of asterisks on the left side,
703:  * with beginning and ending almost-blank lines.
704:  */
707: It’s also important to comment data, whether they are basic types or derived
708: types.  To this end, use just one data declaration per line (no commas for
709: multiple data declarations).  This leaves you room for a small comment on each
710: item, explaining its use.
751:                 (inclass               . +)
752:                 (inher-cont            . c-lineup-multi-inher)
753:                 (knr-argdecl-intro     . 0)
754:                 (label                 . -1000)
755:                 (statement             . 0)
756:                 (statement-block-intro . +)
757:                 (statement-case-intro  . +)
758:                 (statement-cont        . +)
759:                 (substatement          . +)
760:                 ))
761:         (indent-tabs-mode . t)
762:         (show-trailing-whitespace . t)
763:         ))))
765: (dir-locals-set-directory-class
766:  (expand-file-name "~/src/linux-trees")
767:  'linux-kernel)
770: This will make emacs go better with the kernel coding style for C
771: files below ~/src/linux-trees.
772: But even if you fail in getting emacs to do sane formatting, not
773: everything is lost: use indent.
774: Now, again, GNU indent has the same brain-dead settings that GNU emacs
775: has, which is why you need to give it a few command line options.
776: However, that’s not too bad, because even the makers of GNU indent
777: recognize the authority of K&R (the GNU people aren’t evil, they are
778: just severely misguided in this matter), so you just give indent the
779: options -kr -i8 (stands for K&R, 8 character indents), or use
780: scripts/Lindent, which indents in the latest style.
781: indent has a lot of options, and especially when it comes to comment
782: re-formatting you may want to take a look at the man page.  But
783: remember: indent is not a fix for bad programming.
784: Note that you can also use the clang-format tool to help you with
785: these rules, to quickly re-format parts of your code automatically,
786: and to review full files in order to spot coding style mistakes,

FILE submitting-patches.html
196: Submitting patches: the essential guide to getting your code into the kernel¶
197: For a person or company who wishes to submit a change to the Linux
198: kernel, the process can sometimes be daunting if you’re not familiar
199: with “the system.”  This text is a collection of suggestions which
200: can greatly increase the chances of your change being accepted.
201: This document contains a large number of suggestions in a relatively terse
202: format.  For detailed information on how the kernel development process
203: works, see A guide to the Kernel Development Process. Also, read
204: Linux Kernel patch submission checklist
205: for a list of items to check before submitting code.
206: For device tree binding patches, read
207: Submitting Devicetree (DT) binding patches.
208: This documentation assumes that you’re using git to prepare your patches.
209: If you’re unfamiliar with git, you would be well-advised to learn how to
210: use it, it will make your life as a kernel developer and in general much
211: easier.
212: Some subsystems and maintainer trees have additional information about
213: their workflow and expectations, see
214: Subsystem and maintainer tree specific development process notes.
216: Obtain a current source tree¶
217: If you do not have a repository with the current kernel source handy, use
218: git to obtain one.  You’ll want to start with the mainline repository,
219: which can be grabbed with:
220: git clone git://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git
223: Note, however, that you may not want to develop against the mainline tree
224: directly.  Most subsystem maintainers run their own trees and want to see
225: patches prepared against those trees.  See the T: entry for the subsystem
226: in the MAINTAINERS file to find that tree, or simply ask the maintainer if
227: the tree is not listed there.
230: Describe your changes¶
231: Describe your problem.  Whether your patch is a one-line bug fix or
232: 5000 lines of a new feature, there must be an underlying problem that
233: motivated you to do this work.  Convince the reviewer that there is a
234: problem worth fixing and that it makes sense for them to read past the
235: first paragraph.
236: Describe user-visible impact.  Straight up crashes and lockups are
237: pretty convincing, but not all bugs are that blatant.  Even if the
238: problem was spotted during code review, describe the impact you think
239: it can have on users.  Keep in mind that the majority of Linux
240: installations run kernels from secondary stable trees or
241: vendor/product-specific trees that cherry-pick only specific patches
242: from upstream, so include anything that could help route your change
243: downstream: provoking circumstances, excerpts from dmesg, crash
244: descriptions, performance regressions, latency spikes, lockups, etc.
245: Quantify optimizations and trade-offs.  If you claim improvements in
246: performance, memory consumption, stack footprint, or binary size,
247: include numbers that back them up.  But also describe non-obvious
248: costs.  Optimizations usually aren’t free but trade-offs between CPU,
249: memory, and readability; or, when it comes to heuristics, between
250: different workloads.  Describe the expected downsides of your
251: optimization so that the reviewer can weigh costs against benefits.
252: Once the problem is established, describe what you are actually doing
253: about it in technical detail.  It’s important to describe the change
254: in plain English for the reviewer to verify that the code is behaving
255: as you intend it to.
256: The maintainer will thank you if you write your patch description in a
257: form which can be easily pulled into Linux’s source code management
258: system, git, as a “commit log”.  See The canonical patch format.
259: Solve only one problem per patch.  If your description starts to get
260: long, that’s a sign that you probably need to split up your patch.
261: See Separate your changes.
262: When you submit or resubmit a patch or patch series, include the
263: complete patch description and justification for it.  Don’t just
264: say that this is version N of the patch (series).  Don’t expect the
265: subsystem maintainer to refer back to earlier patch versions or referenced
266: URLs to find the patch description and put that into the patch.
267: I.e., the patch (series) and its description should be self-contained.
268: This benefits both the maintainers and reviewers.  Some reviewers
269: probably didn’t even receive earlier versions of the patch.
270: Describe your changes in imperative mood, e.g. “make xyzzy do frotz”
271: instead of “[This patch] makes xyzzy do frotz” or “[I] changed xyzzy
272: to do frotz”, as if you are giving orders to the codebase to change
273: its behaviour.
274: If you want to refer to a specific commit, don’t just refer to the
275: SHA-1 ID of the commit. Please also include the oneline summary of
276: the commit, to make it easier for reviewers to know what it is about.
277: Example:
278: Commit e21d2170f36602ae2708 ("video: remove unnecessary
279: platform_set_drvdata()") removed the unnecessary
280: platform_set_drvdata(), but left the variable "dev" unused,
281: delete it.
284: You should also be sure to use at least the first twelve characters of the
285: SHA-1 ID.  The kernel repository holds a lot of objects, making
286: collisions with shorter IDs a real possibility.  Bear in mind that, even if
287: there is no collision with your six-character ID now, that condition may
288: change five years from now.
289: If related discussions or any other background information behind the change
290: can be found on the web, add ‘Link:’ tags pointing to it. If the patch is a
320: Fixes: 54a4f0239f2e ("KVM: MMU: make kvm_mmu_zap_page() return the number of pages it actually freed")
323: The following git config settings can be used to add a pretty format for
324: outputting the above style in the git log or git show commands:
325: [core]
326:         abbrev = 12
327: [pretty]
328:         fixes = Fixes: %h (\"%s\")
331: An example call:
332: $ git log -1 --pretty=fixes 54a4f0239f2e
333: Fixes: 54a4f0239f2e ("KVM: MMU: make kvm_mmu_zap_page() return the number of pages it actually freed")
338: Separate your changes¶
339: Separate each logical change into a separate patch.
340: For example, if your changes include both bug fixes and performance
341: enhancements for a single driver, separate those changes into two
342: or more patches.  If your changes include an API update, and a new
343: driver which uses that new API, separate those into two patches.
344: On the other hand, if you make a single change to numerous files,
345: group those changes into a single patch.  Thus a single logical change
346: is contained within a single patch.
347: The point to remember is that each patch should make an easily understood
348: change that can be verified by reviewers.  Each patch should be justifiable
349: on its own merits.
350: If one patch depends on another patch in order for a change to be
351: complete, that is OK.  Simply note “this patch depends on patch X”
352: in your patch description.
353: When dividing your change into a series of patches, take special care to
354: ensure that the kernel builds and runs properly after each patch in the
355: series.  Developers using git bisect to track down a problem can end up
356: splitting your patch series at any point; they will not thank you if you
357: introduce bugs in the middle.
358: If you cannot condense your patch set into a smaller set of patches,
359: then only post say 15 or so at a time and wait for review and integration.
362: Style-check your changes¶
363: Check your patch for basic style violations, details of which can be
364: found in Linux kernel coding style.
365: Failure to do so simply wastes
366: the reviewers time and will get your patch rejected, probably
367: without even being read.
368: One significant exception is when moving code from one file to
369: another -- in this case you should not modify the moved code at all in
370: the same patch which moves it.  This clearly delineates the act of
371: moving the code and your changes.  This greatly aids review of the
372: actual differences and allows tools to better track the history of
373: the code itself.
374: Check your patches with the patch style checker prior to submission
375: (scripts/checkpatch.pl).  Note, though, that the style checker should be
376: viewed as a guide, not as a replacement for human judgment.  If your code
377: looks better with a violation then its probably best left alone.
379: The checker reports at three levels:
380: ERROR: things that are very likely to be wrong
381: WARNING: things requiring careful review
382: CHECK: things requiring thought
386: You should be able to justify all violations that remain in your
387: patch.
390: Select the recipients for your patch¶
391: You should always copy the appropriate subsystem maintainer(s) and list(s) on
392: any patch to code that they maintain; look through the MAINTAINERS file and the
393: source code revision history to see who those maintainers are.  The script
394: scripts/get_maintainer.pl can be very useful at this step (pass paths to your
395: patches as arguments to scripts/get_maintainer.pl).  If you cannot find a
396: maintainer for the subsystem you are working on, Andrew Morton
397: (akpm@linux-foundation.org) serves as a maintainer of last resort.
398: linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org should be used by default for all patches, but the
399: volume on that list has caused a number of developers to tune it out.  Please
400: do not spam unrelated lists and unrelated people, though.
401: Many kernel-related lists are hosted at kernel.org; you can find a list
402: of them at https://subspace.kernel.org.  There are kernel-related lists
403: hosted elsewhere as well, though.
404: Linus Torvalds is the final arbiter of all changes accepted into the
405: Linux kernel.  His e-mail address is <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>.
406: He gets a lot of e-mail, and, at this point, very few patches go through
407: Linus directly, so typically you should do your best to -avoid-
408: sending him e-mail.
409: If you have a patch that fixes an exploitable security bug, send that patch
410: to security@kernel.org.  For severe bugs, a short embargo may be considered
411: to allow distributors to get the patch out to users; in such cases,
412: obviously, the patch should not be sent to any public lists. See also
413: Security bugs.
414: Patches that fix a severe bug in a released kernel should be directed
415: toward the stable maintainers by putting a line like this:
416: Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
750: that, if you have your patches stored in a git repository, proper patch
751: formatting can be had with git format-patch.  The tools cannot create
752: the necessary text, though, so read the instructions below anyway.
754: Subject Line¶
755: The canonical patch subject line is:
756: Subject: [PATCH 001/123] subsystem: summary phrase
759: The canonical patch message body contains the following:
762: A from line specifying the patch author, followed by an empty
763: line (only needed if the person sending the patch is not the author).
764: The body of the explanation, line wrapped at 75 columns, which will
765: be copied to the permanent changelog to describe this patch.
766: An empty line.
767: The Signed-off-by: lines, described above, which will
768: also go in the changelog.
769: A marker line containing simply ---.
770: Any additional comments not suitable for the changelog.
771: The actual patch (diff output).
774: The Subject line format makes it very easy to sort the emails
775: alphabetically by subject line - pretty much any email reader will
776: support that - since because the sequence number is zero-padded,
777: the numerical and alphabetic sort is the same.
778: The subsystem in the email’s Subject should identify which
779: area or subsystem of the kernel is being patched.
780: The summary phrase in the email’s Subject should concisely
781: describe the patch which that email contains.  The summary
782: phrase should not be a filename.  Do not use the same summary
783: phrase for every patch in a whole patch series (where a patch
784: series is an ordered sequence of multiple, related patches).
785: Bear in mind that the summary phrase of your email becomes a
786: globally-unique identifier for that patch.  It propagates all the way
787: into the git changelog.  The summary phrase may later be used in
788: developer discussions which refer to the patch.  People will want to
789: google for the summary phrase to read discussion regarding that
790: patch.  It will also be the only thing that people may quickly see
791: when, two or three months later, they are going through perhaps
792: thousands of patches using tools such as gitk or git log
793: --oneline.
794: For these reasons, the summary must be no more than 70-75
795: characters, and it must describe both what the patch changes, as well
796: as why the patch might be necessary.  It is challenging to be both
797: succinct and descriptive, but that is what a well-written summary
798: should do.
799: The summary phrase may be prefixed by tags enclosed in square
800: brackets: “Subject: [PATCH <tag>...] <summary phrase>”.  The tags are
801: not considered part of the summary phrase, but describe how the patch
802: should be treated.  Common tags might include a version descriptor if
803: the multiple versions of the patch have been sent out in response to
804: comments (i.e., “v1, v2, v3”), or “RFC” to indicate a request for
805: comments.
806: If there are four patches in a patch series the individual patches may
807: be numbered like this: 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4. This assures that developers
808: understand the order in which the patches should be applied and that
809: they have reviewed or applied all of the patches in the patch series.
810: Here are some good example Subjects:
811: Subject: [PATCH 2/5] ext2: improve scalability of bitmap searching
812: Subject: [PATCH v2 01/27] x86: fix eflags tracking
813: Subject: [PATCH v2] sub/sys: Condensed patch summary
814: Subject: [PATCH v2 M/N] sub/sys: Condensed patch summary
838: The explanation body will be committed to the permanent source
839: changelog, so should make sense to a competent reader who has long since
840: forgotten the immediate details of the discussion that might have led to
841: this patch. Including symptoms of the failure which the patch addresses
842: (kernel log messages, oops messages, etc.) are especially useful for
843: people who might be searching the commit logs looking for the applicable
844: patch. The text should be written in such detail so that when read
845: weeks, months or even years later, it can give the reader the needed
846: details to grasp the reasoning for why the patch was created.
847: If a patch fixes a compile failure, it may not be necessary to include
848: _all_ of the compile failures; just enough that it is likely that
849: someone searching for the patch can find it. As in the summary
850: phrase, it is important to be both succinct as well as descriptive.
852: Backtraces in commit messages¶
853: Backtraces help document the call chain leading to a problem. However,
854: not all backtraces are helpful. For example, early boot call chains are
855: unique and obvious. Copying the full dmesg output verbatim, however,
856: adds distracting information like timestamps, module lists, register and
857: stack dumps.
858: Therefore, the most useful backtraces should distill the relevant
859: information from the dump, which makes it easier to focus on the real
860: issue. Here is an example of a well-trimmed backtrace:
861: unchecked MSR access error: WRMSR to 0xd51 (tried to write 0x0000000000000064)
862: at rIP: 0xffffffffae059994 (native_write_msr+0x4/0x20)
863: Call Trace:
864: mba_wrmsr
865: update_domains
866: rdtgroup_mkdir
872: Commentary¶
873: The --- marker line serves the essential purpose of marking for
874: patch handling tools where the changelog message ends.
875: One good use for the additional comments after the --- marker is
876: for a diffstat, to show what files have changed, and the number of
877: inserted and deleted lines per file. A diffstat is especially useful
878: on bigger patches. If you are going to include a diffstat after the
879: --- marker, please use diffstat options -p 1 -w 70 so that
880: filenames are listed from the top of the kernel source tree and don’t
881: use too much horizontal space (easily fit in 80 columns, maybe with some
882: indentation). (git generates appropriate diffstats by default.)
883: Other comments relevant only to the moment or the maintainer, not
884: suitable for the permanent changelog, should also go here. A good
885: example of such comments might be patch changelogs which describe
886: what has changed between the v1 and v2 version of the patch.
887: Please put this information after the --- line which separates
888: the changelog from the rest of the patch. The version information is
889: not part of the changelog which gets committed to the git tree. It is
890: additional information for the reviewers. If it’s placed above the
994: <http://www.kroah.com/log/linux/maintainer-03.html>
995: <http://www.kroah.com/log/linux/maintainer-04.html>
996: <http://www.kroah.com/log/linux/maintainer-05.html>
997: <http://www.kroah.com/log/linux/maintainer-06.html>
1000: Kernel Linux kernel coding style
1002: Linus Torvalds’s mail on the canonical patch format:<https://lore.kernel.org/r/Pine.LNX.4.58.0504071023190.28951@ppc970.osdl.org>
1004: Andi Kleen, “On submitting kernel patches”Some strategies to get difficult or controversial changes in.
1005: http://halobates.de/on-submitting-patches.pdf

FILE 2.Process.html
195: how the process works is required in order to be an effective part of it.
197: 2.1. The big picture¶
198: The Linux kernel uses a loosely time-based, rolling release development
199: model.  A new major kernel release (which we will call, as an example, 9.x)
200: [1] happens every two or three months, which comes with new features,
201: internal API changes, and more. A typical release can contain about 13,000
202: changesets with changes to several hundred thousand lines of code. Recent
203: releases, along with their dates, can be found at Wikipedia.
206: [1]
207: Strictly speaking, the Linux kernel does not use semantic versioning
208: number scheme, but rather the 9.x pair identifies major release
209: version as a whole number. For each release, x is incremented,
210: but 9 is incremented only if x is deemed large enough (e.g.
211: Linux 5.0 is released following Linux 4.20).
214: A relatively straightforward discipline is followed with regard to the
215: merging of patches for each release.  At the beginning of each development
216: cycle, the “merge window” is said to be open.  At that time, code which is
217: deemed to be sufficiently stable (and which is accepted by the development
218: community) is merged into the mainline kernel.  The bulk of changes for a
219: new development cycle (and all of the major changes) will be merged during
220: this time, at a rate approaching 1,000 changes (“patches,” or “changesets”)
221: per day.
222: (As an aside, it is worth noting that the changes integrated during the
223: merge window do not come out of thin air; they have been collected, tested,
224: and staged ahead of time.  How that process works will be described in
225: detail later on).
226: The merge window lasts for approximately two weeks.  At the end of this
227: time, Linus Torvalds will declare that the window is closed and release the
228: first of the “rc” kernels.  For the kernel which is destined to be 9.x,
229: for example, the release which happens at the end of the merge window will
230: be called 9.x-rc1.  The -rc1 release is the signal that the time to
231: merge new features has passed, and that the time to stabilize the next
232: kernel has begun.
233: Over the next six to ten weeks, only patches which fix problems should be
234: submitted to the mainline.  On occasion a more significant change will be
235: allowed, but such occasions are rare; developers who try to merge new
236: features outside of the merge window tend to get an unfriendly reception.
237: As a general rule, if you miss the merge window for a given feature, the
238: best thing to do is to wait for the next development cycle.  (An occasional
239: exception is made for drivers for previously-unsupported hardware; if they
240: touch no in-tree code, they cannot cause regressions and should be safe to
241: add at any time).
242: As fixes make their way into the mainline, the patch rate will slow over
243: time.  Linus releases new -rc kernels about once a week; a normal series
244: will get up to somewhere between -rc6 and -rc9 before the kernel is
245: considered to be sufficiently stable and the final release is made.
246: At that point the whole process starts over again.
247: As an example, here is how the 5.4 development cycle went (all dates in
248: 2019):
252: September 15
253: 5.3 stable release
255: September 30
256: 5.4-rc1, merge window closes
258: October 6
259: 5.4-rc2
261: October 13
262: 5.4-rc3
264: October 20
265: 5.4-rc4
267: October 27
268: 5.4-rc5
414: One of the largest mistakes made by kernel developers (or their employers)
415: is to try to cut the process down to a single “merging into the mainline”
416: step.  This approach invariably leads to frustration for everybody
417: involved.
420: 2.3. How patches get into the Kernel¶
421: There is exactly one person who can merge patches into the mainline kernel
422: repository: Linus Torvalds. But, for example, of the over 9,500 patches
423: which went into the 2.6.38 kernel, only 112 (around 1.3%) were directly
424: chosen by Linus himself. The kernel project has long since grown to a size
425: where no single developer could possibly inspect and select every patch
426: unassisted. The way the kernel developers have addressed this growth is
427: through the use of a lieutenant system built around a chain of trust.
428: The kernel code base is logically broken down into a set of subsystems:
429: networking, specific architecture support, memory management, video
430: devices, etc.  Most subsystems have a designated maintainer, a developer
431: who has overall responsibility for the code within that subsystem.  These
432: subsystem maintainers are the gatekeepers (in a loose way) for the portion
433: of the kernel they manage; they are the ones who will (usually) accept a
434: patch for inclusion into the mainline kernel.
435: Subsystem maintainers each manage their own version of the kernel source
436: tree, usually (but certainly not always) using the git source management
437: tool.  Tools like git (and related tools like quilt or mercurial) allow
438: maintainers to track a list of patches, including authorship information
439: and other metadata.  At any given time, the maintainer can identify which
440: patches in his or her repository are not found in the mainline.
441: When the merge window opens, top-level maintainers will ask Linus to “pull”
442: the patches they have selected for merging from their repositories.  If
443: Linus agrees, the stream of patches will flow up into his repository,
444: becoming part of the mainline kernel.  The amount of attention that Linus
445: pays to specific patches received in a pull operation varies.  It is clear
446: that, sometimes, he looks quite closely.  But, as a general rule, Linus
447: trusts the subsystem maintainers to not send bad patches upstream.
448: Subsystem maintainers, in turn, can pull patches from other maintainers.
449: For example, the networking tree is built from patches which accumulated
450: first in trees dedicated to network device drivers, wireless networking,

